# Telegram Analytics and Monitoring

Lux provides transport-neutral Telegram analytics helpers for normalizing Bot
API updates, aggregating chat and audience activity, building rolling monitoring
buckets, evaluating alert thresholds, and producing report payloads.

These helpers do not call Telegram or persist state. They can be wired behind
webhooks, jobs, dashboards, or alerting systems.

## Normalize Updates

```elixir
alias Lux.Integrations.Telegram.Analytics

update = %{
  "update_id" => 1,
  "message" => %{
    "message_id" => 10,
    "date" => 1_800,
    "text" => "/start hello",
    "from" => %{"id" => 101, "username" => "ada"},
    "chat" => %{"id" => -100, "type" => "supergroup"}
  }
}

Analytics.event(update)
```

## Summaries

```elixir
Analytics.summary([update])
```

The summary includes total updates, active chats, active users, message counts,
command counts, media counts, average text length, and frequency maps by chat,
user, message type, and command.

## Rolling Buckets

```elixir
Analytics.buckets([update], 60)
```

Use buckets to feed dashboards, rate monitors, or anomaly detectors.

## Alerts

```elixir
metrics = Analytics.summary([update])

Analytics.alerts(metrics, %{
  max_updates: 100,
  max_callback_queries: 25,
  min_active_users: 5,
  max_commands: 50
})
```

## Reports

```elixir
Analytics.report([update], %{
  chat_id: -100,
  generated_at: DateTime.utc_now() |> DateTime.to_iso8601(),
  thresholds: %{max_updates: 100},
  limit: 5
})
```

Reports are plain maps that can be serialized to JSON, sent to a webhook, or
stored by the application layer.

## Retention

```elixir
Analytics.retention([
  %{period: "2026-05-11", users: [1, 2, 3]},
  %{period: "2026-05-12", users: [2, 3, 4]},
  %{period: "2026-05-13", users: [3, 4, 5]}
])
```
